; SI:%POINTER-UNSIGNED reads a fixnum's 25-bit pointer field as an unsigned
; number (sys/sys/qmisc.lisp): a negative N is N + 2^25.  MIT's added the
; shifted sign bit, whose value is negative (-2^24, so the term is -2^25),
; which made a negative N more negative, N - 2^25: -1 gave -33554433 and
; A-MEMORY-VIRTUAL-ADDRESS, -263168, gave -33817600.
; Check a source change with --compile --files sys/sys/qmisc.lisp
; The first three cases are controls: a non-negative N is itself, and
; SI:%MAKE-POINTER-UNSIGNED, the inverse, gives the negative fixnum for an
; unsigned number from 2^24 up.  System 1003 without the change fails the
; other four.
(si:%pointer-unsigned 5) => 5
(si:%pointer-unsigned most-positive-fixnum) => 16777215
(si:%make-pointer-unsigned (+ (expt 2 24.) 5)) => -16777211
(si:%pointer-unsigned -1) => 33554431
(si:%pointer-unsigned most-negative-fixnum) => 16777216
(si:%pointer-unsigned (si:%make-pointer-unsigned (+ (expt 2 24.) 5))) => 16777221
(si:%pointer-unsigned si:a-memory-virtual-address) => 33291264
